# --- Gateway host allowlist ---
# Requests whose Host header is not listed here are rejected with 421.
GATEWAY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=api-dev.sdkwork.com,api-dev.birdcoder.com,api-dev.noaper.com,api-dev.dtupay.com,localhost:3910,127.0.0.1:3910

# --- Production domains (real DNS + TLS at nginx) ---
# For production traffic through api.sdkwork.com / api.birdcoder.com api.noaper.com /
# api.dtupay.com,api.noaper.com, switch the allowlist and CORS origins to the production
# names (the nginx site docker/nginx/api.sdkwork.com.conf and
# [security].allowedHosts must agree):
#   GATEWAY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=api.sdkwork.com,api.birdcoder.com,api.noaper.com,api.dtupay.com
#   GATEWAY_CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://api.sdkwork.com,https://api.birdcoder.com,https://api.noaper.com,https://api.dtupay.com,https://console.sdkwork.com,app://dsh,app://birdcoder,app://sdkwork,app://dtupay,tauri://localhost,https://servicewechat.com

# --- External PostgreSQL override (production) ---
# Set these to point the container at managed services instead of the compose
# service, and unset/replace the compose dependency wiring accordingly.
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_HOST=db.example.com
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_SSL_CERT_FILE=/run/secrets/sdkwork/api-gateway/postgres-ca.crt

# --- External services mode (existing PostgreSQL + Redis) ---
# To run the gateway against databases/Redis that already exist (no built-in
# postgres/redis containers), start with the external override file:
#
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.external.yml up -d
#
# and configure the connection here. GATEWAY_POSTGRES_HOST may be
# host.docker.internal to reach a service on the docker host (the override
# adds the host-gateway extra host), or any reachable address.
# The external PostgreSQL must already have the canonical database with a
# same-named schema, per environment (create it with
# docker/postgres/external-schema.sql, e.g. once with `-v db=sdkwork_ai_test`
# and once with `-v db=sdkwork_ai_prod`).
#
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_HOST=host.docker.internal
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DB=sdkwork_ai_prod
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_USER=sdkwork_ai_prod
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_SSL_MODE=disable
#
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_HOST=host.docker.internal
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_PORT=6379
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_DATABASE=0
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_USERNAME=
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_PASSWORD=           # leave empty for LAN passwordless
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_TLS=false
# GATEWAY_CLOUDROUTER_REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=cloudrouter
# Set an explicit URL when RTC Redis needs authentication, TLS, or another DB.
# GATEWAY_RTC_STATE_REDIS_URL=redis://host.docker.internal:6379/1

# --- Ubuntu host-native services mode (apt-installed postgresql/redis-server) ---
# Same override file as above; the difference is only where the services run:
# the docker host itself (the Ubuntu server), reached via host.docker.internal
# (docker-compose.external.yml injects the host-gateway extra host). Built-in
# containers stay disabled. Host-side prerequisites:
#   1. PostgreSQL listens beyond 127.0.0.1 (postgresql.conf:
#      listen_addresses = '*') and pg_hba.conf allows the docker bridge
#      networks; the gateway role owns the canonical database + same-named
#      schema (run docker/postgres/external-schema.sql as superuser).
#   2. The `vector` extension must be installed on the host PostgreSQL
#      (knowledgebase module requirement): apt install
#      postgresql-14-pgvector (PG14 on Ubuntu 22.04; pick the matching
#      postgresql-XX-pgvector for the server version, e.g. PGDG repository).
#   3. Redis binds beyond 127.0.0.1 (redis.conf: bind 0.0.0.0, and disable
#      protected-mode or allow the docker bridge) so the container can reach
#      it through the host gateway address.
# Then use the values above with host.docker.internal and the per-environment
# GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DB / GATEWAY_POSTGRES_SCHEMA pair (must be equal).
